\description{
Draws data from a population correlation matrix, given either directly or
built from a factor model. The population correlation is either supplied in
\code{R}, or assembled from a loading matrix \code{Lambda}, the factor intercorrelations
\code{Phi}, and the unique variances \code{Psi} as
\eqn{R = Lambda\, Phi\, Lambda' + Psi}, standardized to a correlation matrix.
By default (\code{marginals = "normal"}) cases are drawn with normal marginals via
a matrix square root of the population correlation (a Cholesky factor, or a
symmetric eigen square root for a positive-semidefinite but singular
population). With \code{marginals = "empirical"} the cases instead reproduce the
population correlation while carrying the empirical marginal distributions of
a supplied data set. With \code{marginals = "VM"} or \code{"IG"} the cases reproduce the
population correlation while carrying non-normal marginals with a prescribed
\code{skewness} and \code{kurtosis}. Setting \code{categories} additionally discretizes the
drawn data into ordered categories, optionally so that the population polychoric
correlation of the categorized data equals the target correlation. Setting
\code{target_rmsea} or \code{target_cfi} perturbs the population with model error, so the
factor model fits it only approximately (a more realistic simulation target).
}
\details{
Provide the population either as a ready correlation matrix in \code{R}, or through
the model components \code{Lambda}, \code{Phi}, and \code{Psi}; the two ways are mutually
exclusive. When the model components are used, \code{Phi} defaults to the identity
matrix (orthogonal factors) and \code{Psi} defaults to the unique variances that
make the population a correlation matrix (\eqn{1 - \mathrm{diag}(Lambda\, Phi\,
Lambda')}); the assembled covariance is standardized with
\code{\link[stats:cov2cor]{stats::cov2cor()}} so a non-standardized \code{Psi} still yields a correlation
matrix. With the default \code{Psi}, a factor model whose implied communalities
exceed 1 (a Heywood case) leaves no unique variance and is rejected; a \code{Psi}
you supply is instead only required to give positive variances and a
positive-semidefinite population.

With \code{marginals = "empirical"}, the data are generated by the iterative
rank-matching algorithm of Ruscio and Kaczetow (2008): the population
correlation is reproduced while each variable takes the empirical marginal
distribution of the matching column of \code{marginal_data} (its values are
resampled with replacement). Only the marginals of \code{marginal_data} are used;
its own correlations are ignored. The reproduction fits \code{n_factors} factors at
each step, defaulting to the number of columns of \code{Lambda} when the population
is built from a factor model. The drawn columns follow the population's
variables, not those of \code{marginal_data}.

With \code{marginals = "VM"}, the Vale-Maurelli (1983) method draws each variable
as a Fleishman (1978) cubic polynomial of a standard normal deviate, with the
cubic's coefficients solved from the target \code{skewness} and (excess) \code{kurtosis}.
The normal deviates are drawn with an intermediate correlation matrix chosen so
the transformed data reproduce the population correlation. Because the Vale-Maurelli
family does not span every valid non-normal distribution (Foldnes & Grønneberg, 2015),
\code{marginals = "IG"} offers an alternative: the independent-generator method (Foldnes &
Olsson, 2016) writes the data as a Cholesky mixture of independent non-normal generators
whose moments are chosen, through the additivity of cumulants, so the mixed
data carry the target marginal moments and the population correlation; the
generators themselves are Fleishman variables. Both accept \code{skewness} and
\code{kurtosis} as a single value (used for every variable) or one value per
variable, defaulting the unset one to 0. Not every (\code{skewness}, \code{kurtosis})
pair is attainable: every distribution needs excess kurtosis of at least
\code{skewness^2 - 2}, and Fleishman's cubic covers a smaller region still, so an
unreachable request is rejected. For \code{marginals = "VM"}, the intermediate
correlation matrix can itself be non-positive-definite; it is rejected unless
\code{force_pd = TRUE}, which projects it to the nearest correlation matrix (via
\code{\link[psych:cor.smooth]{psych::cor.smooth()}}) with a warning.

With \code{categories}, the drawn data are discretized into ordered categories. Each
variable is cut at thresholds placed on the standard-normal scale from the
requested category proportions, \eqn{\tau = \Phi^{-1}(\mathrm{cumsum}(p))}
(Olsson, 1979), and each case is assigned the category its value falls into (an
integer code \code{1} to \code{K}). \code{categories} gives either the number of equally
probable categories (one count for every variable, or one per variable) or, as a
list of proportion vectors, the marginal category proportions per variable. Two
matching modes set what the categorization preserves. With \code{match = "thresholds"}
(the default) the drawn data are cut at these normal-scale thresholds, which
reproduce the requested category proportions exactly for normal marginals and
approximately for the unit-variance \code{"VM"} and \code{"IG"} marginals; because
categorization attenuates product-moment correlations, the categorized data's
Pearson correlation is smaller in magnitude than the population correlation (and,
under non-normal marginals, its polychoric correlation departs from the population
as well). Ordinal output is not available with \code{marginals = "empirical"}, whose
draw keeps the arbitrary scale of \code{marginal_data} rather than the standard-normal
scale the thresholds assume. With \code{match = "polychoric"} the
latent is required to be normal (\code{marginals = "normal"}) and the cases are drawn
multivariate-normal and thresholded, so the population polychoric correlation of
the categorized data equals the target correlation; combining it with non-normal
marginals is a contradiction and is rejected. Categories that a draw leaves empty
are reported with a warning, as empty or near-empty categories destabilize the
polychoric correlation and the factor analysis.

With \code{missing}, missing values are introduced into the drawn data under a chosen
mechanism (Rubin, 1976), each variable holed at a target expected marginal rate
\code{missing_prop}. \code{"MCAR"} draws an independent Bernoulli mask, so missingness is
unrelated to the data. \code{"MAR"} and \code{"MNAR"} set each case's missing probability by a
logistic model, \eqn{\mathrm{logit}(p_i) = a + b\, z_i}, where \eqn{z_i} is the
standardized value of a predictor: another variable for \code{"MAR"} (chosen by
\code{missing_predictor}) or the variable's own value for \code{"MNAR"}. The slope \eqn{b} is
\code{missing_strength}; the intercept \eqn{a} is calibrated per variable so the mean
missing probability equals \code{missing_prop}. The mechanism acts on the drawn (latent)
values, so when \code{categories} also discretizes the data the missing values fall on the
resulting category codes (missingness keyed on the underlying value, not the code).
For \code{"MAR"}, the predictor uses the complete drawn values, so the missingness is at
random with respect to those values even though the predictor column may itself carry
missing values in the returned data. The returned matrix carries the \code{NA}s, which the
correlation estimators handle downstream.

With \code{model_error}, the population is perturbed away from the exact factor structure so the
\code{q}-factor model (\code{q = ncol(Lambda)}) fits it only approximately, at a prescribed misfit;
exact factor structures are unrealistic and overstate recovery in simulation studies
(MacCallum, 2003). The perturbation is applied once, to the population, and every dataset is
then drawn from it; the achieved fit is computed with the same fit-index formulas
\code{\link[=EFA]{EFA()}} uses and returned in the \code{model_error} element. Model error is only applied when a
target is supplied (\code{target_rmsea} and/or \code{target_cfi}); it needs a factor-model population
(\code{Lambda}) with residual degrees of freedom, and is orthogonal to the marginal, ordinal, and
missing-data options (which act on the data drawn from the perturbed population). Three
methods are available. \code{"CB"} (Cudeck & Browne, 1992) adds a residual matrix in the space
orthogonal, in the maximum-likelihood metric, to the model's derivatives, scaled so the
population discrepancy equals \eqn{df \times \mathrm{target\_rmsea}^2}; the \code{q}-factor model
stays the exact minimizer, so the achieved RMSEA equals \code{target_rmsea} to numerical precision
(the CFI follows as a derived quantity). \code{"TKL"} (Tucker, Koopman & Linn, 1969) adds a set of
minor common factors whose spread and size are tuned so the achieved RMSEA -- and,
optionally, CFI -- match the target(s); with a single target the match is close, with both it
is a compromise. \code{"WB"} (Wu & Browne, 2015) draws the population from an inverse-Wishart
distribution centred on the model-implied correlation with precision
\eqn{1 / \mathrm{target\_rmsea}^2}; being a single random draw, its realized RMSEA varies
around the target (and tends to exceed it), so the realized value is reported rather than
guaranteed. CB and WB target the RMSEA only; TKL can target the RMSEA and/or the CFI. The
reported achieved RMSEA/CFI is the misfit of the specified generating model (matching how the
target is defined and how the \pkg{fungible}/noisemaker methods report it); a re-fitted EFA of
the simulated data re-optimizes the loadings and may recover a somewhat smaller RMSEA for TKL
and WB (for CB the generating model is the minimizer, so they coincide). The population must be
an exact factor structure (a diagonal \code{Psi}); a correlated-residual \code{Psi} is not supported.

Replicated draws (\code{n_datasets > 1}) are generated in parallel across
replicates with \pkg{future.apply}; a parallel plan can be selected with
\code{\link[future:plan]{future::plan()}} (the default plan runs sequentially). Each replicate is
assigned its own reproducible random-number stream, so with a fixed \code{seed} the
output is identical regardless of the number of workers.
}
\examples{
# Build a population from a shipped loading pattern and factor correlations
Lambda <- population_models$loadings$baseline
Phi <- population_models$phis_3$moderate

# Draw one normal dataset of 500 cases (the data live in $data)
sim <- efa_simulate(N = 500, Lambda = Lambda, Phi = Phi, seed = 42)
dim(sim$data)

# Return only the population correlation matrix
R_pop <- efa_simulate(Lambda = Lambda, Phi = Phi, return_pop = TRUE)$population

# Draw several datasets at once from a supplied correlation matrix
sims <- efa_simulate(N = 500, R = R_pop, n_datasets = 3, seed = 42)
length(sims$data)

# Reproduce the population correlation but with skewed, empirical marginals
# (here from a chi-squared source with one column per variable)
src <- matrix(rchisq(200 * nrow(Lambda), df = 3), ncol = nrow(Lambda))
dat_emp <- efa_simulate(N = 500, Lambda = Lambda, Phi = Phi,
                        marginals = "empirical", marginal_data = src, seed = 42)

# Draw skewed, leptokurtic data with the Vale-Maurelli method
dat_vm <- efa_simulate(N = 500, Lambda = Lambda, Phi = Phi, marginals = "VM",
                       skewness = 1.5, kurtosis = 4, seed = 42)

# Draw five-category ordinal data whose polychoric correlation matches R
dat_ord <- efa_simulate(N = 500, Lambda = Lambda, Phi = Phi,
                        categories = 5, match = "polychoric", seed = 42)

# Draw data with 15\% missing at random, driven by a neighbouring item
dat_mar <- efa_simulate(N = 500, Lambda = Lambda, Phi = Phi, missing = "MAR",
                        missing_prop = 0.15, seed = 42)
colMeans(is.na(dat_mar$data))

# Add realistic model error: a population the model fits with RMSEA of about .05
# (Cudeck-Browne, the default method; the achieved fit is reported)
sim_me <- efa_simulate(N = 500, Lambda = Lambda, Phi = Phi,
                       target_rmsea = 0.05, seed = 42)
sim_me$model_error$rmsea
}
